## Authentication

The provider sends an API key with every call. Create one in the [Plotwork portal](https://app.plotwork.dev)
under **API keys**, or an organisation key under **Keys and CI**, and set it in the environment:

```shell
export PLOTWORK_API_KEY="pw_..."
export PLOTWORK_ORG="acme"   # only to publish to an organisation's library
```

An organisation key publishes only to its own organisation's library, so set `organization` with one.

## Release channels

| Version | Talks to by default | Get it with |
|---|---|---|
| Stable, such as `0.2.0` | `https://api.plotwork.dev` | `version = "~> 0.2"` |
| Pre-release, such as `0.3.0-beta.1` | `https://api-preview.plotwork.dev` | `version = "0.3.0-beta.1"` (an exact version: a range never picks a pre-release) |

`api_url` or `PLOTWORK_API_URL` overrides the default of either. A key from one environment does not work in the other.

## What leaves your machine

`plotwork_import` sends the text of the files you give it, and `plotwork_render` and `plotwork_diagram`
send diagram source. Plotwork works on it in memory and stores nothing unless `plotwork_diagram`
publishes it. Send configuration, not state or plan files with secrets in them: state can hold passwords,
and the provider cannot tell which values are secret.

## Keeping a link stable

A change to a diagram's source makes a new diagram with a new `url`. Set `alias` on `plotwork_diagram`
for a `stable_url` that never changes and always shows the latest picture: embed that one in a README,
a wiki, or an architecture decision record.

